<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 8 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Great Builders</h1><p class="path-tagline">Rulers who built their worlds in stone, law and cities.</p><p class="path-intro">Some rulers are remembered for what they destroyed; these are remembered for what they raised. This path crosses four millennia of builders, of codes and canals, libraries and basilicas, mosques, palaces and whole capitals, and asks what building meant to power.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Builder</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Person · r. c. 1792–1750 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Hammurabi</span><span class="path-step-why">The Babylonian king whose law code, carved in stone, is among the oldest surviving statements of justice.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person · r. 669–627 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ashurbanipal</span><span class="path-step-why">Assyria's last great king, whose library at Nineveh preserved Mesopotamia's literature for us to find.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justinian-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Person</span><span class="path-step-name">Justinian I</span><span class="path-step-why">The emperor of Hagia Sophia and the Corpus Juris, Rome's law given its final form.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person</span><span class="path-step-name">Charlemagne</span><span class="path-step-why">The Frankish king whose schools and scripts carried learning through Europe's leanest centuries.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mansa-musa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · r. 1312–1337</span><span class="path-step-name">Mansa Musa</span><span class="path-step-why">Mali's pilgrim emperor, whose gold built Timbuktu's mosques and reputation.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shah-jahan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1592–1666</span><span class="path-step-name">Shah Jahan</span><span class="path-step-why">The Mughal of the Taj Mahal, grief made architecture.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-xiv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person</span><span class="path-step-name">Louis XIV</span><span class="path-step-why">Versailles as a machine of rule: the builder-king who domesticated an aristocracy.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person</span><span class="path-step-name">Peter the Great</span><span class="path-step-why">The tsar who built a capital on a swamp to force Russia's face towards Europe.</span></span></a></li></ol>
